        "question": {
          ":question/id": "9550b16f-de7d-518a-a6cb-a76e16773bf9",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/problem": "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=x^{2} + x - 2 y^{2} + 3 y - 3$ that passes through the point $(x,y)=(2,-1)$.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=2 x + 1 \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=3 - 4 y \\\\ \\nabla f(2,-1)&=\\left(5\\right)\\,i+\\left(7\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(2,-1)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(5\\right)^2+\\left(7\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{74}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(2,-1)}{\\|\\nabla f(2,-1)\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(5\\right)\\,i+\\left(7\\right)\\,j}{\\sqrt{74}} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{5 \\sqrt{74}}{74}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{7 \\sqrt{74}}{74}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient.",
