          ":question/id": "b32b5666-3a56-5c5b-b6ab-d5ba3a6ebb5b",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
          ":question/problem": "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=e^{- x + y} \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)}$ that passes through the point $(x,y)=(\\pi,- \\frac{\\pi}{6})$.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Apply both product and chain rules to each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(- \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{- x + y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{- x + y} \\\\ \\sin\\left(\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}\\right)&=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\cos\\left(\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}\\right)&=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\nabla f(\\pi,- \\frac{\\pi}{6})&=\\frac{1}{2 e^{\\frac{7 \\pi}{6}}}\\,\\mathbf v \\\\ \\mathbf v&=\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf v\\|^2&=\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)^2+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=8 \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(\\pi,- \\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|&=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{e^{\\frac{7 \\pi}{6}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude; the common exponential factor is positive and cancels:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(\\pi,- \\frac{\\pi}{6})}{\\|\\nabla f(\\pi,- \\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient.",
